  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA few showers lingered across north-central and far northeast \nWisconsin during the late evening. These showers should come to an\nend overnight. Some patchy fog was also noted across the north.\nIt was mild with temperatures in the upper 40s to lower 50s. \n\nFor Wednesday, a warm front will lift northward allowing\nfor unseasonably mild temperatures for late October with record or\nnear record high temperatures. There is a 60-90% chance temperatures\nreach 70 degrees south of Highway 29. Record highs for Wednesday \n(10/28) are 70 at Green Bay (1946), 71 at Wausau (1986) and 78 at \nRhinelander (1927). Showers and scattered thunderstorms along a \ncold front will move across the area Wednesday evening. Bufkit \nsoundings indicated around 500 J/KG of CAPE although 0-6 shear \nvalues are around 20 knots. However, bufkit soundings indicated \nstrong winds off the deck (40-50 knots). The Day 2 Outlook from \nSPC has the Marginal Risk of severe weather over west-central WI. \nThe showers should come to an end Thursday morning across eastern \nWI as high pressure builds into the region. Dry and mild conditions\ncontinue Thursday and Friday before the next chance of rain \n(15-30%) arrives on Saturday and then lingers into Sunday. Based \non the model output of the Canadian/ECMWF low confidence if/when \nrain would occur next weekend.",
